  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Becoming more unsettled late this afternoon into the\nweekend, with chances for showers, thunderstorms, and localized\nheavy rain.\n\nLatest surface analysis reveals a 1024 mb surface high near Bermuda.\nTo the north, a dampening upper trough and surface low continue to\ncross the St. Lawrence Valley. The associated cold front extends\nsouthwest into the eastern Ohio Valley. Ahead of this boundary,\nsunny skies across the local area have pushed afternoon temperatures\ninto the upper 80s to lower 90s inland ( lower 80s along the \ncoast).\n\nMesoanalysis shows deep layer shear of 20-30 kt (highest NNE).\n500700 J/kg MLCAPE across northern and central VA piedmont \nzones, though a capping inversion (75-100 J/kg CIN) remains \nover much of eastern VA and the MD/VA Eastern Shore per 18z \nanalysis. CAMs continue to show MLCAPE increasing to ~1000 J/kg\nby early evening as the front edges closer to the area, and the  \ntime-lagged HRRR still looks to initiate some loosely organized\nconvection late this afternoon (2022z / 46 PM EDT), focused \nalong a pre-frontal trough just to our NW, aided by a weak \nshortwave crossing the Central Appalachians and pushing into\nnorthern portions of our area this evening. A few organized \nstorm cores capable of localized damaging wind gusts remain \npossible through 10p-midnight, primarily across northern zones.\nSPC has maintained a Marginal Risk (Level 1/5) for this threat.\n\nThe cold front drops south into the region late tonight, with\nthe front stalling across southern VA and NE NC Friday afternoon.\nThe 12z HREF PMM highlights a 30% signal for 3\"/3-hr QPF along \nand south of this boundary. Localized downpours and some\nlocalized flash flood issues remain the primary hazard, focused\nsouth of US-58. The Day 2 ERO has been adjusted east to cover\nmore of the area mainly along and south of US-460 in southern/SE\nVA and northeast NC. Temperatures will feature a relatively sharp\ngradient Friday, ranging from the low 80s north to lower 90s \nsouth.\n\nWidespread precipitation chances peak Saturday, as the stalled\nboundary lifts back north as a warm front, as a couple of weak \nmid-level shortwave disturbances push across the area. High \nprecipitable water values, modest instability (skinny CAPE\nprofile), and weak steering flow should yield efficient warm\nrainfall processes and additional localized hydro concerns \n(supported by an area-wide WPC ERO Marginal Risk). Considerable \ncloud cover will keep a lid on Saturday highs to the low to mid\n80s. The boundary washes out Sunday, leading to noticeably \nlower convective coverage and warming temperatures early next\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Drier early next week as an upper ridge builds into\nthe region, bringing above-normal temperatures.\n\nMostly sunny and dry conditions return Monday and Tuesday as an\nupper-level ridge centered near the Gulf Coast expands northward.\nHighs will return to the upper 80s and lower 90s, with rain chances\nremaining below 20% (isolated late-afternoon shower possible in\nsouthside Hampton Roads and coastal NE NC Monday). Diurnal \nshower and thunderstorm chances gradually tick back up mid-to- \nlate week as heat and moisture continue to build.",
